const TITLE = "Agent Security Policy: Enforcing Integrity and Confidentiality Outside the Model for LLM Agents and Agent Swarms";
const SHORT_TITLE = "Agent Security Policy";

const ABSTRACT = "Tool-using language-model agents can be redirected by untrusted content and may expose information through subsequent model requests. Because instructions, retrieved content, and tool results share the model context, prompt-level defenses alone cannot provide a deterministic enforcement boundary. We present Agent Security Policy (ASP), a declarative runtime policy model that moves authorization and information-flow decisions outside the model, as Content Security Policy moved script restrictions outside the web page. ASP combines a tool-path gateway for authorization, delegated scope, integrity tracking, and swarm-level containment with a model-API egress monitor that enforces confidentiality labels collected through tool interactions. The monitors share an engine-issued agent identity, so policy decisions can account for both an agent's authority and the data its context has accessed. We specify security invariants, implement a reference prototype, and evaluate it through designed attack scenarios, a counterfactual replay of 1,233,716 events from a research dataset of 46 agents, end-to-end tests, latency measurements, and deployment-confinement probes. In a designed indirect-injection scenario, capability withdrawal prevents a payment by an otherwise authorized agent that an allowlist alone permits. In the trace replay, the selected least-privilege policy would have blocked 7.9% of events, and task-scoped confidentiality labels marked 2.1% of model requests, compared with 9.8% under per-agent labels; this is a difference in how many requests carry a label, not a measured reduction in leakage. Task-scoped integrity tracking flagged 43.8% of shared-state writes, compared with 79.1% under per-agent tracking, while still preventing an agent from shedding taint by starting a fresh context. End to end, an agent that read a secret through the tool path was refused on the model path, and in a confined container deployment all 16 bypass probes failed. The in-process gateway decision took 0.047 ms at the median, and resolving an agent's identity added 1.33 ms per model request. These results characterize policy behavior and prototype feasibility rather than measured reductions in real-world harm. ASP's guarantees depend on complete mediation, trusted task lifecycle management, and deployment confinement; we discuss these assumptions and the remaining challenges in provenance tracking, deployment integration, and evaluation.";

