p("For the consensus rows, decisions by this rule are reciprocal-consensus alerts, not blocked events. The thresholds were set on this dataset and should be treated as starting points; Section 8 discusses why they will not transfer unchanged.");
h2("RQ4: Taint Granularity");
p("Integrity tracking is only useful if a meaningful fraction of contexts stays clean. We measured how the unit of taint affects that fraction. In the replay, taint is attached to spans, which in this dataset correspond to individual computer-use or coding sessions. We compared five configurations on the same 101,332 shared-state writes: span-level taint as the replay implements it, agent-level taint in which an untrusted read taints the whole agent for the rest of the day (as the engine-owned and MCP paths do under the default scope), agent-level taint with propagation through chat messages, and task-scoped taint with and without propagation. For task scope, each computer-use or coding session is a task, because the village scaffold, not the agent, starts sessions; chat messages belong to the sender's current task.");
fig("fig4_taint_granularity.jpg", "Share of shared-state writes flagged as coming from a contaminated context, by unit of taint, on the AI Village replay. Task-scoped rows (solid and dotted bars at the bottom) refuse laundering through fresh spans; the span row does not.",
  "Horizontal bar chart of five rows. Span: 43.8 percent. Agent: 79.1 percent. Agent with chat propagation: 92.4 percent. Task, opened by a controller: 43.8 percent. Task with chat propagation: 53.2 percent.", 5.0);
p("Figure 4 shows the result. Span-level taint flags 43.8% of writes but offers no protection against an agent that starts a new span. Agent-level taint flags 79.1%, and a majority of active agents is tainted on 317 of 389 days, a median of 40 minutes after the day's first event (interquartile range 18 to 77 minutes). Adding propagation through chat flags 92.4%; a majority is tainted on 364 days, a median of 20 minutes into the day (10 to 43). With the span-level implementation, enabling propagation has no effect at all, because agents' chat messages are separate spans from the sessions in which they read untrusted content, so the taint never reaches a message. Task-scoped taint flags 43.8% of writes, the same as span-level taint, while refusing the laundering that span-level taint allows; 28,477 of the 60,590 tasks (47.0%) end tainted. With propagation through chat it flags 53.2% of writes, and 64.5% of tasks end tainted, against 92.4% of writes for agent-level taint with propagation.");
p("The village is an extreme case: its agents read the open web as part of their job, and they share broadcast rooms. Still, the measurement makes a design point concrete. Agent-level taint closes the laundering gap in Proposition 2 but saturates quickly in such workloads, and once most contexts are tainted, withdrawing capabilities from tainted contexts amounts to withdrawing them from everyone. Task scope keeps both properties here because the scaffold's sessions are natural task boundaries. A deployment whose tasks run for days, or whose controller opens new tasks carelessly, would lose one property or the other; Section 9 relates this to systems that label data rather than contexts.");
h2("RQ5: Enforcement Cost");
p("We measured per-call latency on four paths, each in three runs (Figure 5 and Table 8). The guard workload has 20 agents making 5,000 decisions, four tool calls for every message, with event timestamps 100 ms apart so that agents stay inside their step budget; tripwire feedback is off, but detection still runs, so the cost includes the Sentinel's graph update. The HTTP guard runs against the real service over loopback, with an RS256-signed session token verified on every request. The MCP workload sends 1,000 calls through a real MCP client: 900 allowed file reads and 100 calls to a deny-listed tool. The baseline sends the same 1,000 reads to the same sample MCP server directly over standard input and output, without the engine. We report the median across runs of each percentile.");
fig("fig5_latency.jpg", "Latency per call on each enforcement path (log scale). Bars show the median p50 over three runs; tick and cross markers show p95 and p99. The hatched bar is the direct MCP baseline without the gateway.",
  "Horizontal bar chart on a logarithmic axis. In-process guard p50 0.047 ms. HTTP guard with token verification 2.24 ms. Direct MCP baseline 1.06 ms. MCP proxy refused calls 2.46 ms. MCP proxy allowed calls 4.06 ms. p95 and p99 markers sit close to each bar.", 5.0);
table("Latency per call in milliseconds (median of three runs)", ["Path", "Calls per run", "p50", "p95", "p99"], [
  ["In-process guard (Algorithm 1 and Sentinel)", "5,000", "0.047", "0.068", "0.084"],
  ["HTTP guard, loopback, token verified per request", "5,000", "2.24", "2.65", "3.07"],
  ["Direct MCP call, no gateway (baseline)", "1,000", "1.06", "1.19", "1.31"],
  ["MCP proxy, allowed call", "900", "4.06", "4.77", "6.02"],
  ["MCP proxy, refused call", "100", "2.46", "2.78", "3.78"],
], { colW: [3.1, 0.9, 0.65, 0.65, 0.7] });
p("The policy decision itself is cheap: 47 microseconds at the median, including graph maintenance. Transport and authentication dominate the other paths. Most of the HTTP guard's 2.24 ms is token verification and request handling, and the MCP proxy adds 3.00 ms at the median over a direct call for allowed calls. Refused calls through the proxy are faster than allowed ones because the engine never contacts the downstream server. Across three runs, p50 values varied by at most 4%. The numbers were measured on the final code, including task-scoped taint; an earlier measurement without it gave p50 values within 5% of these. The recorder confirmed that all 100 refused calls in each run were decided by the deny-list rule and none reached the server.");
h2("RQ6: Confidentiality on the Model Path");
p("*End to end.* We ran the engine and the egress monitor as separate processes. The engine served one MCP session under task-scoped taint with a confidentiality class `credentials` defined by a path pattern. The egress monitor ran in enforce mode with one class, `credentials`, identified only by the engine label and blocked for every destination, and it required engine credentials. Model requests were addressed to a reserved host name that never resolves, so a forwarded request ends in the monitor's upstream error and no traffic left the machine. Table 9 lists the steps.");
table("End-to-end run of both monitors (forwarded means the egress monitor allowed the request and attempted the upstream call)", ["Step", "Action", "Expected", "Observed"], [
  ["E1", "Clean agent sends a model request", "forwarded", "forwarded"],
  ["E2", "Agent reads a secret through the MCP proxy", "label credentials", "label credentials"],
  ["E3", "Same agent sends its next model request", "refused", "refused"],
  ["E4", "Model request without a credential", "refused", "refused"],
  ["E5", "Model request with a forged credential", "refused", "refused"],
  ["E6", "Child spawned from the labelled task sends a model request", "refused", "refused"],
  ["E7", "Owner opens a new task for the agent; request with the new credential", "forwarded", "forwarded"],
  ["E8", "Request with the retired credential of the labelled task", "refused", "refused"],
  ["E9", "Engine revokes the agent; its next model request", "refused", "refused"],
  ["E10", "Engine recorder holds one row per engine-identified decision", "5 rows", "5 rows, 3 refused"],
], { colW: [0.5, 3.6, 1.0, 1.0] });
p("All ten steps behaved as designed. The secret was read through the tool path, where the gateway allowed it; the model path refused what followed. The child agent was refused although it never read the secret, because it was spawned in the labelled task. Opening a new task restored model access for the agent, which is the controller's declassification step, while the retired credential stayed refused. Revocation by the engine cut off the agent's model access. Only the five decisions about engine-identified agents reached the recorder; requests without a verifiable identity have nothing to attach to and stay in the monitor's own log.");
p("*Counterfactual on real traces.* We replayed the AI Village data again with a confidentiality class `credentials` defined by the replay policy's own argument rules for credential-store reads and secret printing. Each in-band action is one model turn, and we count a turn's request as labelled if its context held the label before the turn, which is the context that was sent. Table 10 gives the results for agent-level and task-scoped labels, with and without propagation through chat.");
table("Model requests in the AI Village replay that would have carried a credentials label. A model request is one in-band tool action or chat message by an agent (1,223,724 in all: the 1,233,716 replay events less 9,992 out-of-band messages from the anonymous human and scaffold actor, which are not agent model requests); an agent-day is one agent active on one day (4,797); a task is one scaffold session (60,590)", ["Label unit", "Labelled model requests", "Share of 1,223,724", "Labelled units"], [
  ["Agent", "119,545", "9.8%", "351 of 4,797 agent-days"],
  ["Task (controller-opened)", "25,788", "2.1%", "1,310 of 60,590 tasks"],
  ["Agent, with chat propagation", "515,552", "42.1%", "2,047 of 4,797 agent-days"],
  ["Task, with chat propagation", "64,988", "5.3%", "5,393 of 60,590 tasks"],
], { colW: [2.0, 1.3, 0.8, 1.9] });
p("With task-scoped labels, 25,788 model requests on 165 days came from contexts that had touched a credential store; an egress monitor deciding on labels would have held them. The difference between 2.1% and 9.8% is a difference in how many requests carry a label. It is not a measured reduction in data leakage: the dataset does not record which requests contained a secret, and neither figure counts leaks. Task scope is also only as safe as its task boundaries. If a controller opens a clean task while a secret persists outside the session, in an agent's memory notes, a file it wrote or a message it sent, requests in the new task carry the secret without the label, and the monitor allows them. The village agents keep memory notes across sessions, so the 2.1% may undercount requests whose context held a secret. Per-agent labels would have held 9.8% of all requests, and propagation through broadcast chat would have held 42.1%, which, as with taint, is too coarse to be useful. The numbers measure the cost of letting secrets into a context, not a cost the monitors must pay. Most of the 4,681 credential-store reads extracted a token so that the agent could call an API itself. A tool that performed the call with a scoped token would have kept the token out of the context, and none of those requests would have been labelled. The labels show where that refactoring is needed.");
p("*Cost and detectors.* Resolving the credential added 1.33 ms per model request at the median: 1.96 ms with resolution against 0.63 ms with the same labels supplied in a header, over 1,000 refused requests each, measured at the client. The monitor's own decision takes 29 microseconds at the median on its labelled corpus. Its pattern and keyword detectors classified that corpus of 20 synthetic samples without a miss or a false positive, which is far too small to estimate accuracy. None of the results in this section depend on them: every refusal in Table 9 and every labelled request in Table 10 comes from engine labels.");
h2("RQ7: Confinement and Bypass");
p("The MCP proxy's guarantee rests on the confinement condition of Section 5.4, so we tested a deployment that meets it. We ran the container profile from Section 5.4 with the engine serving one session under task-scoped taint and one agent minted with scope over files, mail, a web fetch tool and a shell tool family whose execute tool is deny-listed. The agent container held a probe that acts as a compromised agent: it has arbitrary code execution inside its container and its own engine-issued credential, and it tries to reach a side effect or more authority without an allowed gateway decision. A control call first checks that sanctioned tool calls still succeed. We ran the same probe image twice: confined, and unconfined, where the agent is also attached to an ordinary network with internet access. Table 11 lists the probes and outcomes.");
